No fix is required to seed or trade the 5M AIN float — that path already works. For the real residual (per-call add/remove caps at ~0.03% of pool), replace the `assert_mul_safe`-then-multiply pattern with a full-width mulDiv. The Move toolchain here is move-language `aptos-v1.3.0` (core/vm_move/Cargo.toml:9-10), which has u256, so add to dex.move:

    fun mul_div(a: u128, b: u128, d: u128): u128 {
        assert!(d > 0, error::invalid_argument(EOVERFLOW));
        let r = ((a as u256) * (b as u256)) / (d as u256);
        assert!(r <= (MAX_U128 as u256), error::invalid_argument(EOVERFLOW));
        (r as u128)
    }

and use it at the five mulDiv sites, dropping the now-redundant pre-checks:
- dex.move:120-123 -> `share_x = mul_div(amount_x, pool.lp_supply, reserve_x)`, `share_y = mul_div(amount_y, pool.lp_supply, reserve_y)`
- dex.move:129-132 -> `used_x = mul_div(liquidity, reserve_x, pool.lp_supply)`, `used_y = mul_div(liquidity, reserve_y, pool.lp_supply)`
- dex.move:174-177 -> `amount_x = mul_div(lp_amount, reserve_x, pool.lp_supply)`, `amount_y = mul_div(lp_amount, reserve_y, pool.lp_supply)`
- dex.move:313-319 -> compute numerator/denominator in u256 and return `(numerator/denominator as u128)`, keeping the `assert_mul_safe(reserve_in, BPS_DENOMINATOR)` intent as a u256-checked bound.
Keep dex.move:111 `assert_mul_safe(amount_x, amount_y)` as-is (or widen `sqrt` to u256) only if 18/18-decimal pools are ever intended to be canonical; with the AIN/WBTC pair it is not binding.

